export const sitePages: SitePage[] = [{"file":"index.html","title":"The record","keywords":"Terry A. Davis, TempleOS, the record, autopsy, listen, glyphs, timeline, sources","text":"Terry A. Davis — the record Skip to content The record Autopsy Listen Glyphs Timeline Sources Read the source Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour December 15, 1969 August 11, 2018 Terry A. Davis He wrote an operating system by himself. Not a shell, not a toy kernel the boot loaders, the compiler, the memory manager, the filesystem, the windowing system, the text-to-music editor, the flight simulator, and the games. Then he put all of it in the public domain and gave it away. This page takes that work apart. Every number here was measured from the TempleOS source tree itself rather than repeated from a second-hand article, and the music in the Listen section is his own, replayed from the notation strings in his code. The record For scale: at the time of its release TempleOS was measured at roughly 121,000 lines. Windows 7 shipped at around 40 million. The gap is not a claim about quality it is a reminder of what one person can hold in their head at once when nobody else is writing any of it. Read all 659 files Every file in the tree, browsable and searchable, with the same per-file line counts used in the charts below. Autopsy The tree was cloned, every file walked, and every line classified as code, comment or blank. Two bundled data files the King James Bible and a PCI device dump are excluded from the totals and reported separately, because counting scripture as source code would be both silly and dishonest. Where the 116,129 lines live Top-level directories in the source tree, by lines of code. Not much of it is commented TempleOS is dense and largely self-describing the source reads as the manual. That is a stylistic consequence of working alone for decades with nobody to explain things to. Forty-eight commits, one author, eleven years The published repository records releases rather than daily work, so this is not a measure of how much he wrote it is a measure of how little ceremony there was. One person, shipping versions of a finished thing. The full tree, scaled by lines Every directory is a horizontal slice; area equals lines of code. The twenty largest files Single-file contribution to the whole. What \"from scratch\" meant here Not a hyperbole. His own credits file states that TempleOS executes no code he did not write, aside from a handful of BIOS calls for configuration and that he wrote the compiler too, so no GCC. That means the C preprocessor, lexer, parser, optimiser and code generator in Compiler/ are his, as is the #include -at-the- command-line workflow and the JIT that compiles a program the moment you run it. Roughly 19,139 lines sit in Compiler/ . That is a real compiler, written by one person, that compiles 50,000 lines in under a second. The parts nobody expects Boot loaders written from scratch, so no GRUB RedSea filesystem, plus FAT32 for interoperability VGA driver into 640 480 with 16 colours, text drawn by hand PS/2 keyboard and mouse drivers DolDoc a markup, layout and rendering system for documents Psalmody a graphical sheet-music editor with playback A JIT with no bytecode anywhere, plus an ahead-of-time mode Self-hosting it compiles a distribution of itself 3D sprites as rotated meshes, not stored as GIFs No networking , deliberately, on the grounds that it is not needed Listen TempleOS had no sound card. It drove the PC speaker a single square-wave voice through a notation string like 6hEqDC5B6CDhE . The parser below is a direct port of Play() from Adam/ASnd.HC , and the pitch comes from his own formula in Kernel/KMisc.HC : 440/32 2 ona/12 , where A4 is ona 60 . These are his compositions, lifted verbatim from the source tree melody and lyric together, exactly as they appear in the files. Below them, the wordless themes from his games and graphics demos, on the same single voice. Voice Loop in TempleOS these run forever until you press a key Stop all The grammar, as he documented it Transcribed from the comment block above Play() . w h q e s whole, half, quarter, eighth, sixteenth t set to two-thirds of the current duration . set to one and a half times the current duration ( tie, placed before the note to be extended 1 7 set octave; middle C is octave 4 M3/4 set the meter A G play a note, optionally with # or b Durations persist until changed, which is why a string can start 6h and stay in half notes for the whole phrase. Tempo is in quarter-notes per second; his default was 2.5, and it climbs with the number. GodSong make God generate music His procedural hymn generator, rebuilt in the browser. The PRNG ( GodBits ) is compiled-only crypto.getRandomValues stands in. The duration and note-selection tables are Terry's. Glyphs TempleOS runs at 640 480 in sixteen colours, which he attributed to a specific instruction. Those constraints are the whole aesthetic: a fixed sixteen-entry palette, an 80 30 grid, and an 8 8 bitmap font stretched twice as tall. The palette, in order Sixteen entries, indices 0 15, exactly as the kernel defines them. There is no anti-aliasing and no second shade of anything. Timeline A short life, and a long single project inside it. Dates below are from his own account and from the commit history of the source tree. Keep reading Five more tributes, each built from the tree like everything else. The Oracle His stopwatch and his 7,569 words, answering again. Anatomy of his compiler One line of HolyC through Lex, PrsExp, OptPass, BackC and Asm. Release notes, in his words Seventy-eight dated entries from Doc/ChgLog.DD. The demo gallery Twenty demos described; TicTacToe, Maze and RainDrops running. His tour, in his voice Sixty-two guided-tour stops from Misc/Tour, verbatim. Sources method What was measured Every figure in the Autopsy section comes from cia-foundation/TempleOS , walked file by file at build time. Lines are split into code, comment and blank by a parser that understands both // and /* */ . The commit counts come from git log on that repository. The songs are the literal Play() string literals found in Demo/Snd , Apps/Psalmody/Examples and Demo/AcctExample/TOS (including “Risen”). The GodSong panel reuses his duration/note tables with a browser PRNG standing in for compiled-only GodBits . The font is the sys_font_std[] table in Kernel/FontStd.HC , decoded pixel by pixel. Excluded from all totals: Misc/Bible.TXT (100,118 lines of King James Version, bundled for scripture search) and Misc/PCIDevices.DD (a 9,430-line device list). Including them would have added 109,548 phantom lines to the total enough to make this page look more impressive than the work actually was. What is cited, not measured The biographical dates and the early life come from his Wikipedia entry and from accounts of his own writing specifically ::/Doc/Credits.DD and ::/Doc/FAQ.DD in the source itself, where he describes writing the whole system over the past 13.9 years (full-time) . For the record: TempleOS is in the public domain, not merely open source. His own credits name the handful of things that were not his a FreeDOS-derived font table, a Cyrillic font taken without permission from Orient Display, an LZW compressor adapted from a magazine article he had implemented years earlier at Ticketmaster, and a heap algorithm adapted from a VAX operating system. He listed all of it. That honesty is part of the work. Built as a tribute. TempleOS and its source belong to the public domain; this page is an independent reading of them."},{"file":"explorer.html","title":"Source explorer","keywords":"Browse all 659 TempleOS source files, search contents, read DolDoc documents","text":"Source explorer — TempleOS The record Autopsy Listen Explorer Timeline Sources Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour TempleOS source loading index Source Reading Copy link Copy text Pick a file. 659 files, 3.4 MB of readable HolyC, assembly and DolDoc every line Terry A. Davis wrote, by hand. Nothing here is generated, and nothing is mine. Compiler/CMain.HC the front door Kernel/KMisc.HC where the pitch formula lives Adam/ASnd.HC the music notation parser Kernel/FontStd.HC the font on the other page Doc/Credits.DD his own credits, in his own words"},{"file":"oracle.html","title":"The Oracle","keywords":"Stopwatch oracle, vocabulary, GodPick, GodWordStr, words","text":"The Oracle — TempleOS Skip to content The record Autopsy Listen Source Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour Adam / God The Oracle The holiest feature in TempleOS is a stopwatch. You watch numbers fly, you press a key to latch one, and the machine answers with words from a 7,569-word vocabulary his vocabulary, in his file, picked by his formula. This is presented as his artifact, not a toy. The timer here runs on your browser clock, not his keyboard timer, and the random bits come from your machine everything else is verbatim. Ask — Press OKAY and the machine will answer. OKAY — latch the timer Ten words Ask again The mechanic, in his code Latching the timer GodPick puts up a timer and waits. When you press OKAY, whatever the keyboard-event clock reads at that instant becomes random input. Adam/God/HolySpirit.HC:33 — his words on the button Picking the word GodWordStr takes that many bits and indexes the loaded vocabulary. That single line is the whole oracle. Adam/God/HolySpirit.HC:138 The vocabulary is Adam/God/Vocab.DD 7,569 words, one per line, exactly as he left it. Read HolySpirit.HC · Read Vocab.DD · His Holy Spirit notes Built as a tribute. TempleOS and its source belong to the public domain; this page is an independent reading of them."},{"file":"compiler.html","title":"Anatomy of his compiler","keywords":"HolyC compiler stages, lexer, parser, optimiser, code generation","text":"Anatomy of his compiler — TempleOS Skip to content The record Autopsy Listen Source Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour Compiler / Anatomy of his compiler He wrote the compiler too lexer, parser, optimiser and code generator, roughly 19,000 lines so TempleOS never touches GCC. Here is what one line of HolyC meets on its way to machine code, stage by stage, in his own functions. Each excerpt is the first function of its stage file, exactly as it appears in the tree. The notes describe the stage, not the man. The pipeline HolyC has no build system and no object files: #include a file at the command line and the JIT compiles it straight into memory. Read the full sources in the Compiler directory . Built as a tribute. TempleOS and its source belong to the public domain; this page is an independent reading of them."},{"file":"changelog.html","title":"Release notes","keywords":"Change log, releases, versions, TempleOS history","text":"Release notes, in his words — TempleOS Skip to content The record Autopsy Listen Source Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour Doc / ChgLog.DD Release notes, in his words Seventy-eight dated entries from his change log releases, fixes, and small victories, exactly as he wrote them. The log Parsed from Doc/ChgLog.DD . Read the raw file Built as a tribute. TempleOS and its source belong to the public domain; this page is an independent reading of them."},{"file":"gallery.html","title":"Demo gallery","keywords":"Demos and games, TicTacToe, Maze, RainDrops, sprite, graphics","text":"The demo gallery — TempleOS Skip to content The record Autopsy Listen Source Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour Demo / The demo gallery Twenty things he built to show off games, solvers, simulations described in his own header comments. Three of them run below, rebuilt for the browser from his logic. The re-creations follow his code where it is visible and say so where they improvise. The rain falls like his rain; the maze solver is mine, the maze is his. Running now TicTacToe Demo/Games/TicTacToe.HC · his board, my opponent His board geometry, his X and O routines, his win check. The opponent plays at random that part is mine. Your move — you are X. New game Maze Demo/Games/Maze.HC · draw with the mouse, then solve “Draw a maze with left bttn. Solve maze starting at right click.” His instructions, verbatim. The depth-first solver standing in for his is mine. Drag to draw walls. Right-click sets the start. Then solve. Solve Clear RainDrops Demo/Games/RainDrops.HC · 8,192 drops, his constant Eight thousand one hundred ninety-two drops, exactly his DROPS_NUM . Yours falls on a smaller sky so the tab survives. The shelf Descriptions are his header comments, trimmed. Line counts measured, as everywhere else. Built as a tribute. TempleOS and its source belong to the public domain; this page is an independent reading of them."},{"file":"tour.html","title":"His tour","keywords":"Guided tour, Misc/Tour, tutorial stops","text":"His tour, in his voice — TempleOS Skip to content The record Autopsy Listen Source Oracle Compiler Changelog Gallery Tour Misc / Tour His tour, in his voice He wrote a guided tour into the system itself scripts that walk a newcomer through the editor, the command line, and the help index. These are his tour stops, verbatim. The stops Spoken by TourPut() in Misc/Tour . Read the scripts Built as a tribute. TempleOS and its source belong to the public domain; this page is an independent reading of them."}];
